I would specifically encode **TreeHacks = S for Founder Scout**, **WDB = S for Berkeley Founder Scout**, and **SkyDeck = C Founder / B Startup**. TreeHacks explicitly centers students building projects during a hackathon, while WDB describes hands-on engineering work for real clients; SkyDeck, by contrast, is open beyond Berkeley and its accelerator targets companies near their first institutional round, so it is more useful for company discovery/verification than proprietary student-founder discovery. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

For Penn, I'd put **PennApps at S for Founder Scout**, while GRASP is **S/A depending on whether you can identify actual student project ownership** rather than merely lab membership. PennApps is explicitly a large collegiate hackathon, while GRASP's public materials expose technical demos, projects, students, and spinouts, making it strong but requiring more entity-resolution work. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

